@@ -0,0 +1,192 @@
+# Ghost preview network traffic
+
+Ghost previews show every player where each builder is about to place a block. This page
+estimates their network cost before and after the rework, shows the arithmetic, and lists what
+players will notice.
+
+These are **estimates, not measurements.** Before shipping, check them in a 12-player test (see
+[Checking the estimate](#checking-the-estimate)).
+
+## What changed
+
+| | Before | After |
+|---|---|---|
+| Who draws ghosts | The server moves one replicated `Part` per player in `Workspace.Ghosts` | Each client draws every ghost in its own local `Workspace.Ghosts` folder. The server keeps only a small table per player |
+| Your own ghost | Arrives from the server, one round trip behind your cursor | Drawn locally every frame from your own cursor |
+| Client to server | `MoveGhost` (reliable `RemoteEvent`), `(blockTypeName, cellPosition)`, **every rendered frame**, even when nothing changed | `SendGhost` (`UnreliableRemoteEvent`), an 8-byte buffer, **only when the cell, block type, or visibility changes**, at most 20 a second, plus one resend a second while nothing changes |
+| Server to clients | Replicated `CFrame` (and `Color`, `Material`, `Transparency`) of each ghost part, reliably, every server frame it changes | `GhostUpdates` (`UnreliableRemoteEvent`), one batch of every ghost that changed, 20 a second, plus a full snapshot once a second |
+| Wire format | String plus `Vector3` | Integer cell indexes: `u8` sequence, `u8` block type, three `i16` cells (`GhostWire.luau`) |
+
+Block placement (`PlaceBlock`, `BlockPlacer`, `BlockPlacement`) was not changed.
+
+## Unit costs used
+
+From the roblox-performance skill's measured table (Studio, server to one client):
+
+| Item | Bytes |
+|---|---|
+| Remote call header | 9 |
+| String argument | about 2 + its length (a 10-character string call costs 21 = 9 + 12) |
+| `Vector3` argument | 13 |
+| `nil` argument | about 1 to 2 (estimate) |
+| Buffer argument | about 3 + its length (a 16-byte buffer call costs 28 = 9 + 19) |
+| One replicated `CFrame` change on an anchored part | 25 |
+
+These were measured on `RemoteEvent`. The "after" numbers assume an `UnreliableRemoteEvent` call
+has the same 9-byte header; that has not been measured.
+
+Per-packet UDP, IP, and RakNet overhead is left out on both sides. That makes the "before"
+numbers look **better** than they were, because sending a message every frame also means many
+more packets.
+
+Workload: 12 players, all building, server at 60 Hz. Block type names are 4 to 6 characters
+(`"Brick"` = 5, so about 7 bytes).
+
+## Before
+
+### Client to server (each builder's upload)
+
+One `MoveGhost` call = 9 + 7 (`"Brick"`) + 13 (`Vector3`) = **29 bytes**. It fired on every
+`RenderStepped`, whether or not the cursor moved. With placing turned off it still fired
+`(nil, nil)`, about 12 bytes a frame.
+
+| Client FPS | Calls/s | Upload per builder |
+|---|---|---|
+| 60 | 60 | 60 x 29 = 1,740 B/s = **1.7 KB/s** |
+| 144 | 144 | 144 x 29 = 4,176 B/s = **4.2 KB/s** |
+| 240 | 240 | 240 x 29 = 6,960 B/s = **7.0 KB/s** |
+
+Server inbound for 12 builders: **20.9 KB/s at 60 FPS, up to 83.5 KB/s at 240 FPS**, which is
+**720 to 2,880 reliable remote calls a second**, each running a handler that writes up to four
+properties.
+
+Two side effects matter as much as the bytes:
+
+- Client-to-server remotes throttle at about 500 calls a second per client, **shared across all
+  `RemoteEvent`s**. At 240 FPS, `MoveGhost` used about half of that budget by itself.
+- All reliable remotes from one client share one ordered stream. A `PlaceBlock` click waited
+  behind every `MoveGhost` queued in front of it.
+
+### Server to each client
+
+The server wrote `CFrame` on every message, but several writes in one server frame replicate as
+one, and writing the same value does not replicate (assumed here; if it does, the numbers below
+go up). So each ghost costs 25 bytes per server frame in which its **cell** changed, capped at
+60 a second.
+
+A builder sweeping the cursor crosses roughly 10 to 60 four-stud cells a second.
+
+| | Per builder, to each client | 12 builders, to each client | Server total out (x 12 clients) |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Slow sweep (10 cells/s) | 10 x 25 = 250 B/s | 3.0 KB/s | 36 KB/s |
+| Fast sweep (60 cells/s, the cap) | 60 x 25 = 1,500 B/s | **18.0 KB/s** | **216 KB/s** |
+
+All of that is reliable property replication, which goes out first in, first out with no
+priority. A burst of ghost moves delays every other replicated change, including newly placed
+blocks. That backlog matches the 300 to 400 ms ping and ghosts jumping in batches seen in the
+12-player test.
+
+## After
+
+### Client to server
+
+One `SendGhost` call = 9 + (3 + 8) = **20 bytes**. It goes only when the ghost changes, at most
+once every 50 ms, plus one resend a second while nothing changes. The rate no longer depends on
+FPS.
+
+| Builder state | Calls/s | Upload per builder |
+|---|---|---|
+| Cursor moving to a new cell every frame (worst case) | 20 | 20 x 20 = **400 B/s** |
+| Cursor still, or placing turned off | 1 | **20 B/s** |
+
+Server inbound for 12 builders: **at most 4.8 KB/s and 240 calls/s** (down from up to 83.5 KB/s
+and 2,880 calls/s). On a still cursor, 240 B/s.
+
+### Server to each client
+
+One `GhostUpdates` batch = 9 + 3 (buffer header) + 4 (tick) + 15 per changed ghost. Batches go
+out at 20 Hz, and only when at least one ghost changed. Each client gets the same batch.
+
+| Changed ghosts in a tick (n) | Batch bytes | Per client at 20 Hz |
+|---|---|---|
+| 1 | 9 + 3 + 4 + 15 = 31 | 620 B/s |
+| 4 | 9 + 3 + 4 + 60 = 76 | 1.5 KB/s |
+| 12 (everyone moving, worst case) | 9 + 3 + 4 + 180 = 196 | **3.9 KB/s** |
+
+Once a second the batch is a full snapshot of every ghost instead of only the changed ones. The
+snapshot replaces that tick's batch, so it adds nothing when everyone is moving, and costs
+196 bytes a second (12 players) when nobody is.
+
+| | Per client | Server total out (x 12 clients) |
+|---|---|---|
+| Worst case: all 12 builders change cell every tick | **3.9 KB/s** | **47 KB/s** |
+| Nobody moving | 0.2 KB/s (snapshot only) | 2.4 KB/s |
+
+Each client also receives its own ghost's entry and ignores it, because one `FireAllClients`
+is cheaper than 12 separate `FireClient` calls. That is 15 of the 180 entry bytes in a
+12-ghost batch, and the numbers above include it.
+
+The server ignores a client's `SendGhost` messages past 30 a second (an honest client sends at
+most 21), so a modified client cannot make the server do more work than that.
+
+## Summary
+
+| | Before (12 builders sweeping fast) | After (worst case) | Cut |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Each builder's upload | 1.7 to 7.0 KB/s, 60 to 240 calls/s | 0.4 KB/s, 20 calls/s | 4x to 17x |
+| Server inbound | 21 to 84 KB/s, 720 to 2,880 calls/s | 4.8 KB/s, 240 calls/s | 4x to 17x |
+| Each client's download | up to 18 KB/s | 3.9 KB/s | about 4.6x |
+| Server outbound | up to 216 KB/s | 47 KB/s | about 4.6x |
+| Reliability | Reliable, shared with `PlaceBlock` and block replication | Unreliable, on its own pool | Ghost traffic can no longer delay placing or block replication |
+| Scaling with FPS | Linear | None | |
+
+The download side scales with players squared (every builder's ghost goes to every client). At
+the after rates, 20 builders all moving costs 9 + 3 + 4 + 300 = 316 bytes x 20 = 6.3 KB/s per
+client, still well inside the roughly 50 KB/s per-client budget. Before, the same 20 builders
+would have been up to 30 KB/s per client, all of it reliable.
+
+A further option, not done: replace each entry's 8-byte `UserId` with a 1-byte slot number the
+server assigns. That would take a 12-ghost batch from 196 to 112 bytes (about 43% less
+download), at the cost of replicating and keeping in sync a slot per player.
+
+## What players will notice
+
+1. **Your own ghost responds faster.** It is drawn locally on the same frame as your cursor
+   moves. Before, it went to the server and back, so it lagged by your ping (300 to 400 ms in the
+   bad test).
+2. **Other players' ghosts glide instead of snapping.** They get a new cell 20 times a second and
+   ease toward it: most of the way in about 0.1 s (a 50 ms time constant), fully settled in
+   about 0.3 s. A ghost moves smoothly from cell to cell instead of jumping, and during a fast
+   sweep it passes between cells rather than stopping in each one. A jump of more than 8 cells
+   (32 studs), or a ghost reappearing after being hidden, snaps.
+3. **Other players' ghosts trail the cursor by about 50 to 150 ms plus ping.** The builder's
+   client can hold a change for up to 50 ms, the server can hold it for up to 50 ms, and the glide
+   adds about 50 ms. Before, the delay was ping plus up to a frame in theory, but under load it
+   was the whole replication backlog.
+4. **A lost packet can leave a ghost in the wrong cell for up to about 1 second.** Updates are
+   unreliable. When the last update before a builder stops is dropped, the once-a-second resend
+   from that builder and the once-a-second snapshot from the server repair it. After an upload
+   outage of several seconds, the server accepts the builder's next message whatever its
+   sequence number once 2 seconds pass with nothing accepted, so the ghost recovers within about
+   2 seconds. A player who just joined sees existing ghosts within about a second.
+5. **Placing a block can feel quicker under load.** Placement logic is unchanged, but
+   `PlaceBlock` no longer waits behind hundreds of ghost messages in the same reliable stream,
+   and placed blocks no longer replicate behind ghost moves.
+6. **The server no longer has ghost parts.** `Workspace.Ghosts` now exists only on each client.
+   No other code in the place read the server's ghost parts.
+7. A ghost aimed more than about 131,000 studs from the origin is hidden, because its cell does
+   not fit an `i16`. The 512-stud aim ray makes that unreachable in normal play.
+
+## Checking the estimate
+
+Run the same 12-player test before and after, with at least a few players building:
+
+- `Stats.DataSendKbps` and `Stats.DataReceiveKbps` on the server and on one client (Developer
+  Console, Network tab, or the Performance Stats bar).
+- Data ping against network ping in the Performance Stats bar. Data ping well above network ping
+  means the reliable queue is backed up, which should no longer happen.
+- One player running at 240 FPS: their upload should stay under about 0.5 KB/s.
+
+Keep the change if the numbers drop by more than the run-to-run noise. If any measured number
+differs from this page by more than about 2x, update the unit costs above with the measured
+ones.
